**`merge(base, override)` behavior:**
- **Deep merge** — nested dicts recurse (`_merge_dicts`), e.g. `merge({'a': {'b': 1, 'c': 2}}, {'a': {'c': 3}}) == {'a': {'b': 1, 'c': 3}}`
- **Replacement** — lists and scalars in the override replace the base value; a dict in the override replaces a non-dict base value (never concatenates)
- **None removal** — an override key with value `None` is popped from the result, no-op if the key is absent from base (`merge({'a': 1}, {'a': None}) == {}`)
- **No mutation / no aliasing** — the result starts from a full deep copy of `base` (dicts and lists via `_deep_copy`), and every value taken from `override` that isn't merged is deep-copied, so no mutable object is shared with either input
- **Type guard** — `ValueError` if either argument isn't a `dict` (subclasses of `dict` are accepted)

